# Line endings are normalised here rather than left to each developer's git config.
#
# This is not tidiness. The `docs` check parses markdown for fenced code blocks, and its first version
# matched a bare `\n` — so a guide saved with Windows line endings matched ZERO fences and the check reported
# success having examined nothing. The check was hardened, but the underlying cause was that nothing in the
# repository said what a line ending is, so two developers could hold genuinely different bytes.
#
# LF in the repository, native in the working tree. Text is detected automatically and the list below is the
# part worth being explicit about, because a wrong guess on any of them is a diff nobody can read.
* text=auto

*.cs        text diff=csharp
*.csproj    text
*.props     text
*.targets   text
*.slnx      text
*.json      text
*.md        text
*.mjs       text
*.js        text
*.yml       text
*.yaml      text
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.ps1       text eol=crlf

# Binary, so git never tries to translate anything inside them.
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.ico       binary
*.snk       binary
*.nupkg     binary
*.snupkg    binary
